-### PART 2 — PRODUCT MOCKUP / PHOTOGRAPHY SPECS
-
-Each asset below includes: shot composition, lighting, labeling/typography treatment, color notes, and emotional intent.
-
-#### Asset 1 — Helm Comparison Shot (Hero Image)
-
-**Composition:** Real boat helm (center console or flybridge, not a studio set — should read as a genuine dash, slightly worn, salt-stained gelcoat visible at frame edges). Two units mounted side by side at the same height, same viewing angle, roughly 18" apart. Camera positioned at the helmsman's eye height, straight-on, slight downward tilt (~10°) to mimic actual sightline while standing at the wheel.
-
-- **Left unit:** OpenPlotter Premium Pre-Assembled, 7" display, in its enclosure, flush-mounted or RAM-mounted.
-- **Right unit:** Garmin ECHOMAP 93sv, 9" display, mounted identically.
-- Both units powered on, both displaying the **exact same NOAA chart** at the exact same zoom level and GPS position — this identical-chart detail is the entire visual argument and must not be faked or approximated; render/composite the same chart tile into both screens pixel-for-pixel.
-
-**Labeling overlay (added in post, not physical placards on the boat):**
-- Beneath left unit, clean sans-serif tag: **"OpenPlotter Premium — $299"**
-- Beneath right unit: **"Garmin ECHOMAP 93sv — $1,100"**
-- Small monospace subtext under both: *"Same NOAA chart. Same GPS fix."*
-
-**Lighting:** Natural overcast daylight, golden-hour-adjacent but not sunset-orange — we want neutral, honest color temperature (around 5600K), not a moody lifestyle glow. Screens should be legible without glare, shot with a polarizing filter to kill reflections on both displays evenly (fairness matters — don't let one unit look better lit than the other).
-
-**Color notes:** Keep the boat's surrounding materials (white gelcoat, stainless rails, blue water in background) as-is; don't grade toward our brand palette here — this image needs to look like documentary evidence, not an ad.
-
-**Emotional intent:** This is the "receipt" image. No copy needed to make the point — a viewer should look at it for two seconds and feel the price gap as an injustice, not a sales pitch. Avoid any staging that feels like a magic trick; it should feel like a photo a customer could have taken themselves.
-
-#### Asset 2 — Tier 2 CNC Enclosure Cutaway (Exploded Technical Diagram)
-
-**Composition:** Isometric cutaway render, cross-section cut vertically through the center of the Tier 2 unit, exploded slightly along the Z-axis (layers pulled apart ~15–20mm each) so each internal layer is independently visible and labeled, in the style of a Northrop Grumman or NASA technical cutaway — precise line weight, no decorative shading.
-
-**Layers to render, front to back:**
-1. Optically-bonded 10" display stack (show the bonding layer as a distinct thin line — this is the detail that visually proves "no air gap")
-2. Custom 4-layer PCB with visible component silhouettes (Pi CM5 module, connectors, NMEA 2000 transceiver chip labeled)
-3. O-ring seal, shown seated in its channel — call this out with its own leader line, since it's the "user-replaceable" proof point
-4. Billet 6061 aluminum shell, shown with visible machining tool marks / mill finish texture, not a smooth CG default material
-
-**Labeling style:** Thin leader lines (0.5pt) in charcoal, terminating in small dots at the point of reference, running out to the right margin where labels sit in monospace type (JetBrains Mono, all lowercase, small caps optional): `optically-bonded display · zero air gap`, `custom 4-layer pcb`, `o-ring seal · user-replaceable`, `6061 billet aluminum · cnc machined`.
-
-**Color notes:** Rendered mostly in grayscale/metal tones (brushed aluminum silver, PCB green or dark navy substrate) with **one accent color** (amber, #E8912D — see Part 3) used only for the leader lines and labels, so the labels pop against the neutral hardware.
-
-**Lighting:** Studio three-point CG lighting, soft key from upper-left, rim light to separate the exploded layers from each other in depth. No environment/background — pure white or very light gray backdrop, technical-diagram style, not lifestyle.
-
-**Emotional intent:** This image exists to make "built to be fixed" a visible, provable claim rather than a slogan. A viewer with any mechanical background should look at this and immediately understand they could open this thing with a screwdriver and know what they're looking at.
-
-#### Asset 3 — Tier 1 Kit Contents Flat Lay
-
-**Composition:** Overhead (top-down, 90°) flat lay, all Full Kit components arranged with intentional grid alignment — not scattered "unboxing" chaos, but not overly sterile either. Think a diver's gear laid out before a dive, or a fly-fisherman's tackle laid out on a dock: purposeful, respectful of the tools.
-
-**Items to include, arranged left to right roughly in assembly order:**
-- Raspberry Pi 5 board (bare, visible chip labeling)
-- 7" sunlight-readable touchscreen
-- GPS HAT (u-blox NEO-M9N) shown separately, then again seated on the Pi
-- Pre-flashed microSD card, small, positioned near the Pi
-- NMEA 0183/2000 cabling, coiled neatly
-- 3D-printed / injection enclosure shell, both halves
-- Small tool included in kit (screwdriver bit or similar)
-- A printed quick-start card with the GitHub QR code visible
-
-**Background:** A genuine NOAA paper chart (a real chart, not a chartplotter screenshot) as the surface beneath all components — should be a recognizable coastal chart with soundings and depth contours visible around the edges of the components, reinforcing "this reads real charts" subliminally.
-
-**Lighting:** Soft, even, diffused overhead lighting (softbox directly above, large diffusion), minimal shadow, so every component is equally legible — no single hero-lit item.
-
-**Color notes:** Let the chart's cream/tan paper tone and blue water tint set the palette; components' natural colors (Pi green PCB, black cables, white enclosure) sit naturally on top. Don't force-tint this image toward our brand blue — the NOAA chart doing the color work IS the brand message here.
-
-**Emotional intent:** "You can build this." Every part should look approachable and finite — a countable, knowable set of objects, not an intimidating pile of electronics. This is the anti-black-box image.
-
-#### Asset 4 — Install Time-Lapse Storyboard (5 Panels)
-
-**Composition:** Five square (1:1) panels displayed left to right in a single strip, consistent camera position and lighting across all five so they read as a true sequence, not five unrelated photos.
-
-1. **Unbox** — hands lifting the assembled unit out of its shipping box, box flaps still visible in frame.
-2. **Mount** — unit being held up to a helm mounting bracket, hand holding a single screw/knob (RAM-mount style, no drilling required for the standard mount).
-3. **Wire** — close-up of the single NMEA 2000 drop cable being connected, hand mid-connection, showing there's exactly one cable to manage.
-4. **Power On** — screen shown mid-boot, OpenPlotter splash/logo visible on the display, ambient boat light reflected in the bezel.
-5. **GPS Lock** — screen showing a locked GPS fix with the boat's actual position on a real chart, small "GPS: 3D FIX" indicator visible on-screen.
-
-**Caption style (beneath each panel, monospace, centered):** `01 · unbox`, `02 · mount`, `03 · wire`, `04 · power on`, `05 · gps lock`
-
-**Lighting:** Natural boat-cockpit daylight throughout, consistent white balance across all five shots (shoot in one session, same time of day) so the sequence feels literally continuous.
-
-**Emotional intent:** Collapse the anxiety of "installing electronics" into something that looks like it takes less effort than parking a boat. No jargon, no toolbox scattered with 40 parts — five clean panels, five clean actions.
-
-#### Asset 5 — GitHub Repo on Laptop, Held on a Boat
-
-**Composition:** Medium shot, someone standing in a boat cockpit or at the helm, holding a laptop at chest/waist height angled toward camera so the screen is legible, GitHub repository page open showing a real, populated file tree (schematics folder, firmware folder, CAD folder, README with commit history visible — needs to look like an actual active repo, not a placeholder).
-
-**Screen content specifics:** Show file names like `openplotter-pcb-r2.kicad_pcb`, `enclosure-tier2.step`, `firmware/main.cpp`, `LICENSE` (visibly an open-source license name like GPLv3 or MIT), and a commit history sidebar with real-looking recent commit timestamps.
-
-**Background:** Open water, horizon line visible behind the subject, boat's own gunwale or rail in the lower frame to anchor the "on a boat" context clearly — no ambiguity that this could be a coffee shop.
-
-**Lighting:** Bright natural daylight, laptop screen brightness pushed up in a screen-recording/composite pass so it's legible against outdoor light rather than looking dim and washed out (a common real-world laptop-outdoors problem — deliberately solve it in the shot).
-
-**Emotional intent:** "It can't be taken away from you." The visual logic: this information exists independently of the company, physically portable, sitting in someone's hands miles from any office or server room. Subject's expression should be neutral/competent, not smiling-at-camera stock-photo energy — someone checking their own boat's schematics, not performing for an ad.
-
-#### Asset 6 — Tier 2 Assembly in US Machine Shop
-
-**Composition:** Mid-shot of a CNC mill actively cutting a 6061 aluminum billet, workpiece clearly recognizable as the early stage of the Tier 2 enclosure (rough block with initial pocket cuts visible). Coolant spray actively visible, chips flying/piling near the cutting head, motion blur on the spindle for a sense of real-time action.
-
-**Framing:** Slightly low angle, looking up and across the mill bed toward the cutting head, so the machine reads as substantial and industrial rather than a small desktop hobby mill (even if the actual shop is small — frame to communicate real manufacturing capability).
-
-**Details to include:** A visible American flag, USA-made signage, or shop identification somewhere in the background (naturally, not staged center-frame) to support the "Built in USA" claim without needing a text overlay to do all the work.
-
-**Lighting:** Practical shop lighting (overhead fluorescent/LED high-bay), plus the cutting head's own task light if present — should feel authentic to an actual machine shop, slightly cool color temperature, not glamorized.
-
-**Color notes:** Let the aluminum's natural silver and the blue coolant read naturally; this is one of the few images where a touch of our teal/amber accent naturally already exists in the environment (coolant tint, safety signage) — no need to force it.
-
-**Emotional intent:** Tactile proof of "Built in the USA" — the viewer should almost hear the mill and smell the coolant. This is a texture/craft image, meant to sit opposite the clean CG cutaway (Asset 2) as its physical, sensory counterpart.
-
-#### Asset 7 — Founder Speaking to Camera, Underway
-
-**Composition:** Handheld or lightly stabilized (gimbal, not a locked tripod — should feel a little alive), founder standing at the helm or seated near it, boat underway at low idle/no-wake speed, speaking directly to camera. Framed medium-close, boat's wake and shoreline/horizon visible behind them.
-
-**Setting specifics:** No branded backdrop, no ring light, no lapel mic wire hidden — if a mic is visible, that's fine, it reinforces "no studio." Natural ambient boat sound (engine idle, water) should be present in the audio, not scrubbed clean.
-
-**Wardrobe/setting:** Whatever the founder actually wears to run their boat. No branded polo, no styled "founder casual" — this shot fails if it looks art-directed.
-
-**Lighting:** Whatever natural light exists at time of shooting — the entire point is that this is not lit. If shot near dusk/dawn for practical reasons, don't grade it into a moody cinematic look; keep it looking like a phone-adjacent, real camera capture.
-
-**Emotional intent:** "No VC" as a felt experience, not a claim. Contrast this deliberately against every polished Kickstarter founder video with a ring light and a whiteboard — the roughness IS the credibility signal here.
