-### PART 4 — INFOGRAPHIC SPECS
-
-#### Graphic A — Tier Comparison Table
-
-**Layout:** Two stacked comparison blocks, not one flat table, since Tier 1/2 pricing logic differs from Tier 3.
-
-**Block 1 — Tier 1 (three columns):** `Bring Your Own Pi — $99` / `Full Kit — $199` / `Premium Pre-Assembled — $299`. Rows: GPS HAT, Pre-flashed SD card, Enclosure + STLs, Raspberry Pi 5, 7" Touchscreen, Cables, Assembly, 24hr Burn-in Test, Plug-and-Play. Use ✓ (deep green) / ✗ (muted gray, not red — absence isn't a failure, it's a tier boundary) / "You supply" (monospace italic) where relevant. Price shown as a large amber callout number at the top of each column, not buried in a row.
-
-**Block 2 — Tier 2, single wide block below:** `Built in the USA — $549–$749`. Listed as a feature stack rather than a comparison (nothing to compare it against in this graphic): Custom 4-layer PCB, CM5 module, CNC billet aluminum IP67 enclosure, 1000-nit optically-bonded display, Lifetime repair guarantee, Published schematics. Visual treatment should look more premium/technical than Block 1 — darker background panel, amber price callout larger, small aluminum-texture background swatch behind the block.
-
-**Block 3 — Founders Circle, horizontal band beneath both:** Full-width horizontal strip, $500 to $50,000 shown as a gradient scale left to right (amber to teal gradient, symbolizing "money to mission"), with reward icons (Founders Wall, Feature Voting, Private Chat, Revenue-Share, Tax-Deductible Donation) as small labeled icons positioned along the band at their approximate price points. Small text beneath: "No equity offered or implied."
-
-#### Graphic B — "How Your Money Is Spent" Pie Chart
-
-**Chart type:** Donut chart (not full pie — leave the center open for a large "$299" total label), for the Premium Pre-Assembled unit specifically.
-
-**Segments (label each with both % and dollar amount inside/adjacent to the slice):**
-- BOM (components) — largest segment, ocean blue
-- Assembly & labor — teal
-- Duty / freight / import — muted gray
-- Platform fees (Kickstarter + payment processing) — charcoal
-- Margin — amber, deliberately not hidden or minimized in size — the honesty is the trust-builder, so margin should be visibly present, not a sliver
-
-**Center label:** `$299` in Montserrat Bold, with `Premium Pre-Assembled` in small monospace beneath it.
-
-**Callout note beneath chart, monospace, small:** `Pi 5 pricing increased ~57% over the past year due to DRAM shortages. This breakdown reflects current component costs, not last year's.`
-
-**Emotional intent:** No slice should look "hidden" or artificially small — the entire value of this graphic is that margin is shown plainly rather than obscured, so it must not visually undersell itself.
-
-#### Graphic C — Stretch Goal Progress Bar
-
-**Layout:** Single horizontal bar (thick, like a battery/fuel gauge), left to right, with four milestone markers as vertical tick marks along the bar at their proportional position (not evenly spaced — spaced proportionally to dollar value if bar represents $0–$500k+).
-
-**Milestones (each gets a small icon + short label above/below its tick mark, alternating above/below to avoid crowding):**
-- `$75,000` — NMEA 2000 plugin polish
-- `$150,000` — 10" screen option for Tier 2
-- `$300,000` — EU CE certification
-- `$500,000` — Radar integration roadmap
-
-**Fill treatment:** Bar fills left to right in amber as funding progresses; unfunded portion in light gray outline only. Milestones already reached get a small green checkmark badge; unreached milestones stay in monospace gray text.
-
-**Emotional intent:** Should read like a fuel gauge or a fundraising thermometer crossed with a technical roadmap — utilitarian, not celebratory/confetti-styled, consistent with the brand's "plain language" ethos.
-
-#### Graphic D — Risk Matrix (2×2)
-
-**Layout:** Standard 2×2 grid, X-axis = Likelihood (Low → High), Y-axis = Impact (Low → High), quadrant backgrounds subtly tinted (bottom-left palest, top-right most saturated in muted red) so severity is legible even before reading labels.
-
-**Ten risks to plot as labeled dots (position approximate, adjust based on actual internal risk assessment):**
-1. Raspberry Pi 5 supply shortage — (Med likelihood, High impact)
-2. CNC machine shop lead-time slip (Tier 2) — (Med, Med)
-3. Touchscreen panel vendor delay — (Low, Med)
-4. Import/customs delay on sub-components — (Med, Low)
-5. Firmware bug delaying initial ship — (Med, Med)
-6. GPS HAT vendor discontinuation — (Low, Med)
-7. Shipping carrier damage/loss rate — (Low, Low)
-8. Enclosure IP67 seal failure in QC — (Low, High)
-9. Underestimated assembly time per unit — (Med, Low)
-10. Currency/tariff shift affecting Tier 2 cost — (Low, Med)
-
-**Dot styling:** Each dot is a small filled circle in charcoal with a thin amber ring; label text in monospace positioned with a short leader line so labels don't overlap in dense areas (quadrant 2, medium-medium, will likely be crowded — stagger label distance from the dot as needed).
-
-**Emotional intent:** Should look like an actual internal planning document that got published, not a marketing graphic pretending to be transparent — slightly dense, slightly technical, unpolished in a way that reads as credible.
